<sn><md>### Useful aliases

```
git config --global alias.changed "status -s"
git config --global alias.past "log --pretty=format:\"%C(magenta)%h%C(reset): %s - %C(blue)%cr%C(reset)\""
git config --global alias.hist "log --pretty=format:\"%C(magenta)%h%C(reset): %s - %C(blue)%cr%C(reset)\""
git config --global alias.put "commit -m"

git config --global alias.unstage "restore --staged --"
git config --global alias.discard "restore --"


git config --global alias.br branch
git config --global alias.now "branch --show-current"
git config --global alias.home "switch main"

git config --global alias.delta '!f() { br="$1"; shift; git diff --name-status "$br" -- "$@"; }; f'
git config --global alias.bring '!f() { src="$1"; shift; git restore --source="$src" -- "$@"; }; f'


git config --global alias.aliases "config --get-regexp ^alias\\."

```</md></sn><sn><md>#### .gitconfig

last saved git config.

**how to open**

Go to terminal and type these command

```
subl ~/.gitconfig
# if sublime is not installed then
sudo nano .gifconfig
```


`.gitconfig`
```text
[user]
	name = em0n
	email = nazmulhossan2043@gmail.com
[alias]
	
       # basic things
	changed = status -s	
	past = log --pretty=format:\"%C(magenta)%h%C(reset): %s - %C(blue)%cr%C(reset)\"
	hist = log --pretty=format:\"%C(magenta)%h%C(reset): %s - %C(blue)%cr%C(reset)\"	
	put = commit -m	
       
       # Undoing and cleaning
        unstage   = restore --staged --
        discard   = restore --	
	
       # branch related	
	br = branch	
	now = branch --show-current
	home = switch main
	
       # delta and pull from other branch
	delta = "!f() { br=\"$1\"; shift; git diff --name-status \"$br\" -- \"$@\"; }; f"
	bring = "!f() { src=\"$1\"; shift; git restore --source=\"$src\" -- \"$@\"; }; f"
	
       # others
	aliases = config --get-regexp ^alias\\.
[credential]
	helper = store
[diff]
	tool = smerge
[difftool "smerge"]
	cmd = smerge \"$LOCAL\" \"$REMOTE\"
```</md></sn><sn><md>#### Git `bring` 

sometimes we have to restore some folder or files or other branch.In this case the command is

```bash
git restore --soruce=branch -- path/
```

**Alias**
```bash
git config --global alias.bring '!f() { src="$1"; shift; git restore --source="$src" -- "$@"; }; f'
```
+++ read
- Aliases with `!` run from the repository’s top-level directory, not your current subfolder. So use paths relative to the repo root (e.g. App/), or you’ll hit “pathspec did not match” when running it from inside a subdirectory.
- `src=$1` store first argument
- `sift` remove the first argument
- "$@" holds what’s left
+++

##### Explanation

```bash
git bring main App/ Config/file.php
# $1 = main
# after shift: "$@" = App/ Config/file.php

# so this :
git restore --source="$src" -- "$@"

# becomes
git restore --source=main -- App/ Config/file.php

```

+++ **Why the quotes matter**

The quotes keep each argument intact, even if it contains spaces. If you ran:

```bash
git bring main "My Folder/" App/
```

then `"$@"` expands to two arguments: `My Folder/` and `App/`. Without the quotes, the shell would split `My Folder/` into `My` and `Folder/`, which breaks.

**Related variables**

| Variable | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `$1`, `$2`, ... | The first, second, ... argument |
| `$@` | All arguments, kept as separate words (use as `"$@"`) |
| `$*` | All arguments, but `"$*"` joins them into one single string |
| `$#` | The number of arguments |

Rule of thumb: when you want to pass along "all the arguments" to another command, use `"$@"` with the quotes.

